Use `Export documents` to generate PDFs from NAHPU records: specimen labels,
tissue tags, catalog sheets, field books, and other formatted printouts. For a
tabular data file instead, use [Export records](../export-records).

## How the pieces fit together

```text
Record data + Template = one populated label or document block
Populated blocks + Print layout = pages
Pages + preview/export action = PDF
```

{% documentlayout /%}

| Piece | Decides | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Document template** | What one label or block shows, and how it looks | A 50 × 20 mm skull tag |
| **Print-layout preset** | Paper size, margins, grid, ordering, and page breaks | 40 skull tags per A4 sheet |
| **Records** | The values that populate the template | The specimens you select |

Document templates and print-layout presets are saved settings you reuse. Keep
one layout per recurring print job—such as museum labels, tissue tags, or field
sheets.

## Quick export

{% steps %}

1. Open the project `Dashboard` menu and select `Export documents`.
2. Choose a `Layout profile` {% ref id="A" /%}. If no print layouts exist yet,
   select `Load defaults` to add bundled layouts or `Manage presets` {% ref id="E" /%}
   to create one.
3. Review the page setup, template blocks, and selected records.
4. Select `Preview` {% ref id="C" /%} and generate a preview, inspecting the
   first and last pages.
5. Enter a `File name` (optionally turn on `Append date`) and choose a
   destination folder on desktop.
6. Select `Export documents` {% ref id="D" /%}. Once finished, use `Share`,
   open the folder, save a copy, or select `Export another`.

{% /steps %}

## Create a template {% #create-a-template %}

For a full walkthrough from blank canvas to printed sheet, see
[Tutorial 4: Create a mammal voucher tag](/en/tutorials/export/voucher-tag/).

Open `Settings` > `Documents` {% ref id="E" /%}, switch to `Templates`, and
select `Create new template`:

{% steps %}

1. Enter a unique `Template name` and an optional `Description` (up to 80
   characters).
2. Choose the `Record type`: `Specimen`, `Specimen parts`, `Symbionts`,
   `Site`, `Collecting event`, `Narrative`, or `No records`.
3. Choose `1 sided` or `2 sided`. Two-sided templates keep separate Front and
   Back designs; switching to one-sided keeps the Back design saved without
   printing it.
4. Set width and height to the dimensions of one label, tag, or block—not the
   paper sheet.
5. Add text, images, lines, and shapes on the canvas. Select any element to
   adjust position, size, styling, visibility, locking, and stacking order.
6. Select `Save template` (or `Save as...` for a variation).

{% /steps %}

Use the template settings button in the editor to change a saved template's
name, description, or side count. Renaming automatically updates every print
layout that references it.

The template's record type determines which fields and records are offered. For
specimen templates, choose the taxon filter before picking taxon-specific
fields.

## Fill a template with record data

Text elements display record values using placeholders such as
`[specimen::fieldNumber]` mixed with fixed text. Use `Insert Field` rather than
typing keys manually:

```text
[taxonomy::genus] [taxonomy::specificEpithet] - [specimen::fieldNumber]
```

Prefer full `table::field` keys in reusable templates; short keys like
`[fieldNumber]` can resolve to the wrong table when two tables share a column
name.

Three text-element settings control formatting:

| Setting | Use it to | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Text type` and `Format` | Format dates, coordinates, numbers, sex symbols, lists, nested tables, or decoded codes | `17 Feb 2016`, `♀`, `Scrotal` |
| `No Content Placeholder` | Choose what prints when a field is empty: blank, `N/A`, `None`, the field key, or custom text | `[specimen::preparator??N/A]` |
| `Conditional output` | Bracket, replace, or add text based on field conditions | `[82.5]` for an estimated weight |

- `Nested List` prints related records (such as specimen parts or personnel) as
  a table or card list.
- `Encoded Text` converts stored numeric codes into labels. Uncertain sex keeps
  its question mark in every format: `Female?`, `F?`, `♀?`.
- Set `No Content Placeholder` before finalizing layout spacing, because blank
  space and `N/A` take different widths.
- Build conditionals with the `Conditional output` dialog and preview both a
  matching and a non-matching record.
- `Replace text` rewrites values after formatting—ideal for repeated
  abbreviations, whereas conditionals handle changes driven by another field.

See [Export expressions](../export-expressions) for the complete reference,
including [Conditional output](../export-expressions#conditional-output) and
[Text types and their formats](../export-expressions#text-types-and-their-formats).

## Design for real records

Test previews with your longest text, largest repeated lists, missing optional
fields, and long identifiers—short sample records hide overflow.

For narratives or nested lists:

- turn on dynamic text sizing so text boxes expand as needed;
- turn on `Auto-fill page` on the layout block when block heights vary;
- leave adequate wrapping width and printer padding;
- regenerate the preview after any template or layout edit.

For static images, use a managed image path on the device and verify it after
importing the template elsewhere; a template JSON stores the reference, not a
copy of external image files.

## Create a print-layout preset {% #create-a-print-layout-preset %}

Open `Settings` > `Documents` and switch to `Print layouts`. Create a new
layout or use `Save as...` before modifying a working one. Enter an optional
description (up to 80 characters) under the preset name and select `Update` to
save both; the description appears in the preset list.

### Page setup

Configure `Page size` {% ref id="B" /%}, `Orientation`, custom dimensions if
needed, and page padding. Page setup describes the physical sheet; the template
describes one block.

### Template blocks

Add one or more `Template blocks`. For each block, set:

- `Template`: the reusable document template;
- `Copies`: copies printed per record;
- `Rows` and `Cols`: fixed sheet grid;
- `Auto-fill page`: measure each block's actual height instead of using fixed
  row heights;
- template padding: margin around each block;
- `Order by` and `Direction`: deterministic record sorting;
- `Page break after`: force a page break after the block.

If a block shows `This template no longer exists`, pick a replacement template
before previewing or exporting; NAHPU never substitutes one silently.

### Multiple blocks

When a layout contains multiple blocks, `Multiple blocks mode` controls print
order:

- `Continuous (Block-by-block)`: prints all records for block 1, then all
  records for block 2 (best for multi-section packets).
- `Alternate (Record-by-record)`: prints block 1 and block 2 for record 1, then
  for record 2 (best for paired outputs that stay together).

## Practical layout recipes

| Goal | Recommended settings |
| --- | --- |
| One full-page document | One block, 1 row, 1 column, fixed height |
| Fixed label grid | Exact rows and columns, `Auto-fill page` off |
| Variable narrative sheet | Dynamic text sizing and `Auto-fill page` on; preview long records |
| Multiple copies of each label | Increase `Copies` on the block |
| Two-sided tag | `2 sided` template; verify front/back mirroring |
| Mixed packet | Multiple blocks with `Continuous (Block-by-block)` |
| Paired record output | Multiple blocks with `Alternate (Record-by-record)` |

## Preview and export safely

Preview runs the exact same template, measurement, and pagination engine as PDF
export. Before exporting:

- select representative records for each block;
- inspect both the first and last pages;
- verify long text, repeated lists, grid alignment, padding, and both duplex
  sides;
- regenerate the preview whenever it is marked stale.

Changes made to the selected layout on the export screen save back to that
layout—use `Save as...` first when testing variations. Exported PDFs are not
structured data exports or restorable backups.

## Fonts

Open `Settings` > `Documents` > `Fonts` to manage fonts available to templates,
grouped into fonts bundled with NAHPU and fonts you installed. The preview pane
shows regular, bold, and italic styles at your chosen size.

- Select `Install font` to install a `.ttf`, `.otf`, or `.ttc` file from your
  device.
- Select `Find fonts` to browse fonts in your browser, then install the
  downloaded file. You are responsible for font licenses.
- Installed fonts stay on this installation and are not transferred with user
  configs. Install the same font on other devices or pick a replacement when
  importing a template.
- A font referenced by any saved template cannot be deleted until no template
  uses it.

## Import and share templates and layouts

Export templates from `Settings` > `Documents` > `Templates` and layouts from
`Print layouts`. Both support `Export this ...` (single item) and
`Export all ...` (all items) using the same file format, imported through the
same `Import` action. Layout exports include linked templates by default; turn
off `Include linked templates` to export layouts alone.

How conflicts and missing resources are handled on import:

- **Duplicate template names:** prompts to replace or rename (adding numbered
  suffixes when importing several at once).
- **Duplicate layout names:** assigns a non-conflicting name automatically.
- **User-config import:** replaces selected sections atomically.
- **Missing fonts:** lists missing fonts and lets you preview and pick
  replacements before saving, so templates never store unrenderable fonts.
- **Static images:** referenced by filename only; templates with logos warn on
  export and require re-adding the image on the target device.

Because layouts reference templates by name, transfer matching templates and
layouts together. For a complete device copy including project data and managed
files, use `Backup database`.

## Printing specimen tags {% #printing-specimen-tags %}

Printing tags from NAHPU instead of writing them by hand:

- **Saves field time:** attach a small temporary tag with key IDs—such as a
  tissue number (`B-729113`) and field number (`JPO 7299`)—and print full tags
  later at the museum.
- **Improves legibility:** produces uniform, easy-to-read labels regardless of
  handwriting.
- **Prevents transcription errors:** localities, coordinates, and elevations
  are entered once per site and corrected in one place.
- **Allows pre-print curation:** clean up coordinates, elevations, terminology,
  and spelling before printing.

### Tag-printing workflow

NAHPU prints tags directly from templates and print-layout presets. When you
need specialized external typesetting, you can still pair NAHPU exports with an
external label tool:

{% steps %}

1. Export specimen records from NAHPU as a `.csv` file.
2. Clean the data: spellcheck, fix errors, and combine or split columns as
   needed.
3. Load the CSV into
   [Entomolabels](https://labels.entomo.pl/) (Windows only) with your custom
   tag template.
4. Export front and back tag PDFs, then interleave front and back pages.
5. Print on an auto-duplex printer.

{% /steps %}

### Printing equipment {% #printing-equipment %}

- **Printer:** The NAHPU team has used the
  [Epson WorkForce Pro WF-3820](https://epson.com/For-Work/Printers/Inkjet/WorkForce-Pro-WF-3820-Wireless-All-in-One-Printer/p/C11CJ07201)
  with
  [DURABrite® Ultra instant-dry pigment ink](https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B08DX9DNSJ/ref=ppx_yo_dt_b_search_asin_title?ie=UTF8&th=1),
  printing over 1,000 bird specimen tags (spring 2024) for the Louisiana State
  University Museum of Natural Science.
- **Paper:**
  [100% cotton, acid-free 250 gsm paper](https://www.redrivercatalog.com/browse/aurora-art-white-250.html).
  Because this stock is thick and rigid, gently flex sheets before loading so
  the duplexer flips pages reliably.

### Archive the printed output

Keep the generated PDF—and the template and layout presets needed to reproduce
it—with your project archive. Inspect a test printout before running a full
batch, print on durable archival stock, and store physical copies with the
collection records. Printed documents never replace a database backup, project
export, or structured record export; see
[Archiving a project](../#archiving-a-project).

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | What to check |
| --- | --- |
| Placeholder prints literally | Check the template record type and use the full `table::field` key. |
| Conditional prints literally | Expression syntax is invalid—check for missing `]]`, unquoted values, or mixed `&&` and `\|\|`. |
| Condition never matches | Comparisons are case-sensitive, test stored values rather than display labels, and blank values never match ordinary operators. |
| Preview is empty | Check the block record type and select matching records. |
| Template is missing | Reassign the block or import/restore the named template. |
| Text overlaps or clips | Turn on dynamic text sizing and `Auto-fill page`, or enlarge the block. |
| Unexpected blank space | Check fixed rows, template size, page padding, and template padding. |
| Records out of order | Set `Order by` and `Direction` on the block. |
| Unexpected duplicate labels | Check `Copies` and the selected record list. |
| Blocks in wrong sequence | Change `Multiple blocks mode`. |
| Back side missing or rotated | Check `2 sided`, Back content, and mirror settings. |
| Preview is marked stale | Regenerate the preview after your latest edit. |
| Config import changed many settings | Selected-section user-config import replaces each selected section (except `Custom fields`, which merge). |

Every template editor control is documented under
[Export expressions](../export-expressions#template-editor-options). For
developer-level architecture details, see
[Templates and presets](../../../contributing/code/templates-and-presets) and
[Export workflows](../../../contributing/code/data-export-workflows).
